A||||12||RobertVol||IAC replaced||||5||
Z||000000||RobertVol||05-01-2000||09:03 PM||||I replaced the Iac today since getting a code 35. check engine was on. After replacing it the idle was high ..about 1500-1800 rpms. I took it out on the road and drove it hard  to air it out a little. After coming back the idle a set back to 900-1000. No problems. anyway ...my question is after running over 40MPH consistently do I have to disconnect the battery still and reset the ECM ? will by ECM still try to comp for the previous error?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||05-02-2000||02:15 AM||||The error "code" is the only thing that gets stored. If you disconnect the battery it will erase this code if you do not erase it the code will just stay in memory. Stored codes themselves have no effect on the workings of the engine. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-02-2000||10:52 AM||||Oreif,<P>Sorry guy. Stored codes Always effect the running of the engine. The specific code determines how bad the effect is.<P>Rob,<P>Always reset the ECM to clear a code after a repair. This also lets you know for sure when a fresh code has set.<P>Don't be suprised if the 35 was to reappear.  Especially if you didn't check very thuroghly for vacuum leaks. The IAC is a pretty tough little device.<P>All,<P>Stored codes will make the ECM run in one of 2 failure modes.<P>mode 1, the worst, runs the car in open loop at all times. Very bad. Good way to ruin a catalyst asthe engine will nearly always be rich in open loop. The car usually runs like a complete turd in this mode. This mode is used if certain sensors die.<P>mode 2 the car runs in closed loop but can't learn it's fine tuning. You'll get cruddy gas millage, and often squirly performance issues. This mode is sneaky because the car will often appear to run normally but it really isn't. All stored codes will at the very least set the ECM in this mode and keep it like this untill cleared.<P>Stored codes can also cause conditions that result in other bogus codes being set later on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||05-02-2000||12:50 PM||||OK good wasnt sure. Tonight when I get home I will reset the ECM. I will say it was running better after putting the IAC in. Wasnt as jerking on acceleration. But then that was after running it a few miles. But better safe than sorry. will take the suggestion. Thank You  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||mshill||05-02-2000||01:32 PM||||Ogre,  great info.  I get a code 32 about every third week.  The check engine light comes on and then is off again when I start the engine up again.  The code is stored, so does that mean the ECM is not able to go into the "learn" mode to make the engine run more efficiently?  That could explain why my gas mileage is not as good as it should be, cause I don't bother resetting the ECM after every code 32.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gbv||05-02-2000||02:01 PM||||ogre,<P>how can you tell for sure if its running in open loop all the time? can it do this even with no codes stored?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||05-02-2000||02:47 PM||||Ogre, Sorry but according to the Pontiac service manual the mode 1 and mode 2 is only set by a bad sensor DURING the faulty condition. Not what is stored in the ECM memory. It's like storing a number into memory on a calculator, Only used when requested. If vehicle is in mode 1 or mode 2 the SES light will be on during the failing condition. Mode 1 SES on all the time it is a Hard failure and puts vehicle in closed loop mode, sensor input ignored, fixed spark advance, closed loop timer bypassed and no new trouble codes are stored. Mode 2 SES on until fault goes away if fault goes away SES light turns off 10 seconds later. <P> As for the "learning" mode to reset the learning mode on ANY GM ECM you must first "drive car until operating temperature, Then disconnect battery for 10 seconds, To "teach" the vehicle, proceed to drive at part throttle with moderate acceleration and idle conditions until normal performance returns" This is directly out of the Service Manual word for word.<P>To see what mode the vehicle is in, ground out the ALCL then turn ignition on.<BR>Open Loop ~ SES flashes 2.5 times per second<BR>Closed Loop ~ SES flashes Once per second.||||reg||4
Z||000007||theogre||05-02-2000||08:08 PM||||Oreif, don't believe everything the manual says. I'm have hundreds of pages of manual corrections and updates in the draw next to me and that is only for the 87 manual.<P>I've watched ECMs in real time. If there is a stored code learning is disabled and stays so untill the ECM is reset. It will still go into closed loop as soon as the O2 is ready.<P>Like I said.... mode one when certain sensors <I>die</I>. like dead O2 or MAP. Car runs like hell in this mode. Your right. the light stays on in this mode.<P>A spent most of the last 2 winters in mode 2 trying to find the intermitant cause of repeated code 45. (only happened under particular weather conditions. real pain to find. Still not sure I got it since this winter was so mild.) The light did it's little 10 second thing..... then the car idle was off, acceleration was off, the ECM scanner showed closed loop operation as normal but learn was disabled at all times.  (I finally found the MAT sensor that the majority of books say no Fiero 4 cyl uses. It was cooked. I replaced it but I haven't had the right weather to see if that was finally it.)[MAT is only in DIS 4's]Even with the scanner tracking this down was a trud. I finnally replaced the MAT just because it was the only thing I could think of.(I've had the ALDL cover off so long I've lost the damn screws.....)<P>Ideally that is the prefered way to restart learning. Reason is to give the ECM a chance to reset the IAC and stuff like that after repairs. That prevents false errors poping up 2 days after the customer leaves the shop. (We all know how pissed we get when we think we fixed something only to see new codes later..... We usually want to lynch someone.) GM put that in to avoid customer complaints, and maybe get the slagging dealers to test drive the damn cars after they fix them. Hell at most dealers you're lucky if they reset the thing at all.<P>In truth the car will start learning new data anytime the ECM is reset and it never actually stops learning as far as I can tell.<BR>So if you move some place a few thousand feet higher or lower than where you are, the car will over the next few days, likely by the end of the drive there, adjust itself to the thinner/thicker air and run happily even without reseting the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-02-2000||08:19 PM||||...... I'm amazed that last one got posted. IE crashed trying to download acrobat reader about 5 seconds after hitting send. The joys of a world owned by microsoft.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RobertVol||05-02-2000||09:40 PM||||Let me say that I disconnected the cable off the battery for just couple of seconds and re attached just to "see" and then after Resetting my stupid radio stations <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> I got back in the car and drove it around a couple of miles and the thing uhhh "My 2nd baby" i mean runs beautiful @@@ perfect idle. <P>Thanks for all the info guys. ya'll are great||||reg||14
Z||000010||DRH||05-02-2000||09:58 PM||||oreif and ogre,<P>You guys could both be right. All V6 and 84-86 4 had the older ECM (C3 ?). The 87-88 4 (DIS) motor used the newer ECM (P4 ?). It has a much faster processor. I would assume it has more memory and more sophisticated programming also.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||RobertVol||05-02-2000||10:59 PM||||Mine was an 88GT 28k mls. 2.8 Stock||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||05-03-2000||10:06 AM||||DRH, You've got a good point. I haven't had much chance to look at the older ECM's. I'm not sure if the DIS motors have the P class ECM or not but it's definatly faster than the older ones. <P>They appear to all behave about the same in mode 2 from what I have seen. But they likely don't have as much learn capacity and what they do have is less flexible.<P>The reset procedure Oreif listed is the best one for all ECM's. That method insures false crud that is learned after a repair is removed from the system. ||||reg||1
